      body: `Uranus's interior, magnetic field, and ring-moon system are not isolated phenomena — they are deeply interconnected expressions of the planet's unusual history and structure. The rocky core feeds heat upward into the icy mantle, but the mantle's stable compositional gradient traps that heat rather than allowing it to convect outward, explaining Uranus's anomalously low thermal emission. Within the icy mantle, elevated pressures ionise water and ammonia, creating an electrically conducting ionic ocean layer that acts as the dynamo generating Uranus's exotic magnetic field. That field — tilted 59° from the rotation axis and offset from the planet's centre — wobbles dramatically as Uranus rotates every 17.2 hours, sweeping the rings and moons through a time-varying magnetic environment. The rings and moons themselves, locked in Uranus's tilted equatorial plane, respond to this wobbling magnetosphere through charged-particle interactions and orbital dynamics. Follow the connections between these layers below to see how a single planet — likely reshaped by one catastrophic ancient impact — produced one of the Solar System's most distinctive and scientifically puzzling systems.`,
